================================================================================
Materials Project (test fixtures)
================================================================================

Tests/DiffractoCoreTests/Fixtures/mp-import/input-*.json are decoder inputs in
the Materials Project API's response format, copied from the applications' tests:
input-aluminium-v3.json is a recorded API response for mp-134, and the
magnesium and silicon files are smaller records in the same format. They are
credited as Materials Project data, licensed CC BY 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Changes: none to the recorded
response; the other two were written by hand in its format.

    Source: The Materials Project, https://materialsproject.org
    A. Jain et al., "Commentary: The Materials Project: A materials genome
    approach to accelerating materials innovation", APL Materials 1, 011002
    (2013), doi:10.1063/1.4812323

================================================================================
Test fixtures: every source and its licence
================================================================================

Tests/Fixtures/README.md lists each committed fixture file. By source:

CIF files (Tests/DiffractoCoreTests/Fixtures/*.cif). None is a copy of an
entry in a crystallographic database: no file comes from the Crystallography
Open Database (COD), the American Mineralogist Crystal Structure Database
(AMCSD) or ICSD. Each file says where it comes from in its first comment line:
  - written in the tests of mac4DSTEM 7448401b (GPL-3.0) and copied with that
    line, some completed or reduced as the comment says;
  - written for this kit as regression cases (GPL-3.0-only, this licence);
  - corundum_P1_rhombohedral.cif and magnesium_P63mmc_origin_shifted_loop.cif
    were written with pymatgen CifWriter (MIT); the output of a tool carries no
    licence of the tool.
Lattice parameters and atomic coordinates in them are published measurements,
cited as facts. 2H-WS2 (ws2_2H.cif) takes its cell and z(S) from W. J.
Schutte, J. L. de Boer and F. Jellinek, J. Solid State Chem. 70, 207-209
(1987). COD entry 5910003 holds the same structure; COD dedicates its data to
the public domain under CC0 (https://www.crystallography.net/cod/), but the
fixture is written by hand and is not that file.

Oracle outputs (the *-pins.json files). These are numbers computed by the
scripts under tools/pins/, which call the reference packages below; no source
code or test data of those packages is copied into them:
  - pymatgen 2026.9.24 / pymatgen-core 2026.10.16 (MIT) and spglib 2.8.0
    (BSD-3-Clause): cells, symmetry operations, conventional cells, presets,
    the Materials Project decoder and the X-ray cross-check;
  - orix 0.15.0 (GPL-3.0-or-later): orix-*-pins.json and the orix part of
    trigonal-pins.json and symmetry-pins.json;
  - py4DSTEM f050d207 (GPL-3.0): py4dstem-*-pins.json, acom-matcher-pins.json
    and the py4DSTEM part of the orientation-symmetry pins;
  - NumPy and SciPy (BSD-3-Clause), or plain Python: the ring calibration,
    ring indexing, ellipse, ring-overlay and indexing-display pins, and the
    structure-factor phase sum.
No fixture is taken from the test data of pymatgen, orix or py4DSTEM.

Materials Project records (mp-import/input-*.json): CC BY 4.0, credited in
the section above.
